/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  hydrocyclone01_quartz_willsTable91

  A HYDROCYCLONE ON A DECLARED CURVE, held to the printed worked example of
  Wills & Napier-Munn (2006), pp. 215-216 and Table 9.1: a quartz slurry,
  feed pulp 1670 kg/m3 (63.7 % solids), underflow 1890 and overflow
  1460 kg/m3, sieve analyses of both products.

  THE BOOK'S ARITHMETIC, which this case runs in the other direction:
    dilutions (water/solids): feed 0.57, underflow 0.34, overflow 1.00;
    water balance 0.57 F = 0.34 U + (F - U)  ->  U/F = 0.652 (p. 215);
    R = 65.2 x 0.34 / (100 x 0.57) = 0.39 (p. 215);
    column 8, % of each size to the underflow; d50 = 177.5 um.
  HERE: the corrected curve (eq. 9.15 inverted on column 8) and R = 0.39 are
    DECLARED, and the unit recomputes the split, both products' size
    analyses and their dilutions with Kelsall's bypass a_k = R + (1 - R) c_k
    (Wills eq. 9.15 written forward).
  WHAT THIS CASE IS, AND IS NOT: a RECONSTRUCTION, not a prediction and not
    a validation.  Both declared inputs were derived from Table 9.1 itself,
    so the two `anchor` rows of `expected` return the book's own inputs:
    the underflow dilution 0.34 is the number R = 0.39 was computed from
    (through U/F), and the declared curve puts the actual partition of the
    148-209 um class at 0.39 + 0.61 x 0.1803 = 0.49998, so d50 lands in that
    class by construction.  They are an IDENTITY CHECK that the engine's
    bookkeeping (eq. 9.15 forward, the water split, the ln-x interpolation)
    is the book's, and nothing more.  The one number the case COMPUTES that
    was not typed in is the solids split U/F = SUM a_k w_k = 0.65568,
    against 0.65204 from the book's own column 8 summed over the same feed
    (0.652 printed): its +0.56 % is the cost of writing the -74 um class's
    negative corrected recovery as 0, measured -- not a prediction error,
    because no input here is independent of the table.
  WHAT IT CANNOT REPRODUCE, and why: the book's -74 um class reaches the
    underflow LESS than the water does (37.2 % against 39 %), so its
    corrected recovery would be negative; it is written 0, and the recomputed
    underflow carries 0.54 % of the feed more than the book's (see
    `expected`).  That is Kelsall's assumption failing on real data, shown,
    not hidden.
  INPUTS taken: 100 t/h of solids (the book gives F only as a symbol); the
    quartz is the catalogue's silica record (rho_p 2650 kg/m3; the book
    says 2700 -- only the printed C_V and pulp density read it, and no
    anchor does).
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
application   choupoSolve;
description   "A hydrocyclone on a declared corrected curve and water split (Kelsall's bypass), run forward on the worked example of Wills & Napier-Munn (2006) Table 9.1: a quartz slurry, R = 0.39, underflow 65.2 % of the solids at dilution 0.34, overflow at 1.00.";
verbosity     3;
citations
(
    "Wills, B.A.; Napier-Munn, T.J. (2006) Wills' Mineral Processing Technology, 7th ed. Elsevier, Oxford. Chapter 9, pp. 213-216, eqs. 9.14-9.15, Table 9.1."
    "Kelsall, D.F. (1953) A further study of the hydraulic cyclone. Chem. Eng. Sci. 2, 254-272 (as cited by Wills & Napier-Munn 2006)."
);
